    "created_at": "2017-07-04T10:20:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-07-04T10:21:14Z",
    "body": "I suppose that this means that some elements that accept both phrasing and non-phrasing content (like `div`, `section` etc.) can be broken into several paragraphs if something non-phrasing appears between its phrasing-content children. E.g. given the following markup:\r\n```\r\n<div>\r\n  <em>Something</em> <mark>phrasing-content</mark> only\r\n  <p>A non-phrasing sibling</p>\r\n  <b>Some</b> <span>phrasing-content</span> <i>siblings</i> again\r\n</div>\r\n```\r\nthere would be 3 paragraphs inside the `div` — one explicitly marked up with a `p` element (which is non-phrasing, but allowed inside `div`), and two implicitly created from the runs of phrasing content before it and after it, respectively.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
