    "created_at": "2017-07-04T13:10:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-07-04T13:10:35Z",
    "body": "> Does this mean a run of phrasing content within `<p>` won't form a paragraph?\r\n\r\nI suppose that, technically, yes. Paragraphs, explicit or implicit, can occur only in the context where non-phrasing content is expected. Only the `p` element as a whole creates a paragraph, but not its content nor any part of it per se. Paragraphs can't be nested (except in some very special cases like with `object`). Also, if an element that accepts _only_ phrasing content is \"interrupted by other types of content\", it means wrong markup, but the algorithm describes the correct behavior.\r\n\r\nSo I believe that the \"element that accepts content other than phrasing content\" part is necessary here, but maybe it can be reworded for clarity. Also, I see another problem in this definition: does it mean that metadata content between phrasing content (e.g. a `link` element that is allowed in `body`) would break the implicit paragraph? I suppose it shouldn't.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
