    "created_at": "2018-01-30T09:10:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-01-30T09:10:00Z",
    "body": "While the concept of the \"paragraph\" in the HTML sense is quite clear, it seems that people often tend to think of the paragraphs as \"logical/thematic containers\" and try to nest things like lists in them. Maybe the note explaining this should be moved from the `p` element definition to the general section about the paragraph concept?\r\n\r\nIt also seems that the significant number of people is not very comfortable with new HTML5 kinds of content and still tries to categorize the HTML elements with the HTML4-style \"block-level\" vs. \"inline\" dichotomy. This confusion has gone so far that the W3C HTML5.2 now has a note that \"inline blocks, inline tables, as well as floated and positioned block-level elements... cannot be children of the `p` element\", and [this addition is listed in the \"Changes\" section](https://github.com/w3c/html/issues/1112), apparently implying that _before_ that change these things were _allowed_ in `p` and increasing the confusion instead of clarifying it.\r\n\r\nI suppose that part of the problem with new kinds of content is that we don't have a short term to replace the \"block-level elements\" concept. While \"inline elements\" have been more-or-less replaced with the Phrasing content, the \"block-level elements\" can't be described better than \"Flow content except Phrasing content\", which is long and might sound scary for the newcomers.\r\n\r\nMaybe it would make sense to introduce a special term for that subset of Flow content? It might be called \"Paragraph content\", which would make it clearer that elements of this kind can't be contained in the elements with \"Phrasing content\" content model, and that a paragraph is the \"minimal structural unit\" of such content and thus can't contain other paragraphs. If this idea is not too crazy, I'd like to open a new issue for it for both WHATWG and W3C specs.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
