      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2018-01-30T12:44:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-01-30T12:44:03Z",
    "body": "It seems that people try to nest lists inside paragraph because they misunderstand the concept of the paragraph in HTML (e.g. that any list item itself forms a separate paragraph). Wouldn't it be useful to present the general rule more clearly in the spec, rather than provide exceptions for each particular case? Other \"paragraph-making\" elements, such as `address`, have the same limitation as well...\r\n\r\nI noticed the W3C spec issue not as a justification, but as an example that the confusion about \"block-level element\" (and implication that CSS styling can \"convert\" elements from one category into another and therefore affect their content model) still exists and is quite popular.\r\n\r\nRegarging use cases for short name for \"Flow content that is not Phrasing content\", I'd say that saying \"The `p` element can't contain _X_ content\" can be the simplest answer to the questions about incorrect `p` nesting and somehow it's easier to memorize than \"the `p` element can't contain anything that is not _Y_ content\".",
    "author_association": "NONE",
